WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:11.800
Fajar sends his manager a careful update: three hundred words, in the order things happened.

2
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:20.300
She reads two lines and replies: so, are we on track? What she understood is what he communicated.

3
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.400
A message can fail in five ways.

4
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:30.900
Burying the lead: telling the story in order, so the point arrives last.

5
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:38.200
The curse of knowledge: once you know something, you skip the steps your reader needed.

6
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:43.900
Wrong altitude: detail when they wanted the summary, or the reverse.

7
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.000
Monologue: sending, and never checking what landed.

8
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.300
And stakes freeze: fluent with friends, rigid when it matters, because it was never rehearsed.

9
00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:01.400
Look at his message. The line that matters is the last one.

10
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:07.100
At work, people want the point first and the journey second.

11
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:14.400
So he rewrites it. The point, the reason, and the decision he needs from her.

12
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.700
You can audit any message with three questions. Does the first sentence carry the point?

13
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.600
Could a smart outsider follow it without your context?

14
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.900
And did you ask anything that checked what they understood?

15
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:36.000
What they understood is what you communicated.

16
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.100
Put the point first, the journey second.

17
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:49.000
Now it is your turn. Take the last important message you sent and score it against the five challenges.
